{"format":"tvf-scene","v":1,"name":"example · one premise","loop":48,"ease":"inout","hold":0,"gravity":1,"wind":0,"trail":null,"camera":[{"t":0,"box":[-0.709953488,-0.165348837,-0.328186047,0.0509023256]},{"t":0.2,"box":[-0.709953488,-0.165348837,-0.328186047,0.0509023256]},{"t":0.23,"box":[-0.343257153,-0.133954828,-0.0753474977,0.133954828]},{"t":0.4,"box":[-0.343257153,-0.133954828,-0.0753474977,0.133954828]},{"t":0.43,"box":[-0.0234001047,-0.163054988,0.262424407,0.0489360349]},{"t":0.6,"box":[-0.0234001047,-0.163054988,0.262424407,0.0489360349]},{"t":0.62,"box":[-0.0280883721,-0.167743256,0.313995349,0.0352046512]},{"t":0.8,"box":[-0.0280883721,-0.167743256,0.313995349,0.0352046512]},{"t":0.83,"box":[0.418604651,-0.133953488,0.770232558,0.133953488]}],"stops":[0.2,0.4,0.6,0.8],"beats":[{"title":"A column slid along its line","text":"From [a place](#lit:2), take [one direction](#lit:0) and the thin [cone of directions](#lit:1) about it. Put [a column of matter](#lit:4) in it and slide it out and back. Four times as far, it is sixteen times as broad, and a pull falling as one over the distance squared loses sixteen times: [its pull](#lit:3) is the same wherever along the line it sits.\n\nAt the powers [half a step less](#lit:5) and [half a step more](#lit:6) it nearly doubles or halves (1.91 and 0.52 here). A direction's pull depends only on how much matter lies along it, not where, under the inverse square and no other law."},{"title":"A shell from inside","text":"Stand [inside a shell](#lit:7), [off its middle](#lit:8). Every [line through the place](#lit:9,10,11,12) meets the shell at its two ends, and meets it at the same slant at both. So the thin cone of directions along it takes [a piece of shell](#lit:15,16) at each end, the farther piece larger by just as much as it is farther.\n\nEach direction and its opposite carry equal columns, and [their pulls](#lit:17,18,23,24) cancel, [line after line](#lit:19,20,21,22): [no pull](#lit:47) inside. This is Newton's own argument, Proposition 70 of the *Principia*."},{"title":"A ball, its light and its pull","text":"Light keeps the same premise: a surface's brightness per direction stays the same along a ray. So [a ball](#lit:28) seen from [a place](#lit:25) gives [light](#lit:30) as its brightness times [the share of directions it fills](#lit:26,27), and [a pull](#lit:31) as that same share times one amount, its density times four thirds of its radius.\n\nMove it out and both fall together. [The pull over the light](#lit:32) has no distance in it."},{"title":"A cube parts them","text":"Put [a cube](#lit:29) face on in its place. Its light is still its brightness times [the share its near face fills](#lit:26,27), but its pull now takes columns of different lengths, and how they weigh changes with distance.\n\nFrom half a side off to twenty sides off, [the pull over the light](#lit:32) rises by about three quarters. Only a round body keeps the two on one share."},{"title":"A lens reads the column","text":"Light passing [a mass](#lit:35) is bent by [the column along its line of sight](#lit:34), laid out on the sky. [The rays](#lit:36,37,38,39,40,41) bend by the pull's own law one dimension down: only [the column inside a ray's closest approach](#lit:42) bends [it](#lit:43), the sky's own shell theorem.\n\nWhat a look sees bent is a column's pull, carried to it as directions. [The essay, §15](https://github.com/reportbase/wander/blob/main/papers/essays/33-one-stage-two-plays.md)"}],"path":null,"items":[{"fill":{"color":"#f4f1ea","opacity":1},"stroke":null,"width":0,"open":false,"exact":{"x":[-0.9,0.9,0.9,-0.9],"y":[0.313953488,0.313953488,-0.313953488,-0.313953488],"r":null,"smooth":null,"ctrl":null,"holes":[]}},{"morph":0},{"morph":1},{"morph":2},{"morph":4},{"morph":3},{"morph":5},{"morph":6},{"morph":7},{"morph":9},{"morph":10},{"morph":11},{"morph":12},{"morph":13},{"morph":15},{"morph":14},{"morph":16},{"morph":17},{"morph":18},{"morph":19},{"morph":21},{"morph":20},{"morph"